  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nHeat and Thunderstorm Chances Sunday: The ridge looks to flatten\na bit and should allow for a front to sag into the area along\nwith shortwave energy. Latest guidance suggests that a front \nand perhaps some ongoing elevated storms may be ongoing early \nSunday morning to our northwest. Our environment on Sunday looks\nto be similar to the previous few days however mid level temps \nshould begin to cool. This cooling aloft, along with the \nforcing of the front should allow for the development of \nafternoon/evening showers and storms. We will need to assess the\nmesoscale environment in future updates for exact storm hazards\nhowever sufficient instability and weak shear would support a \npulse severe setup with damaging winds the most likely hazard. \nIf storm motions are slow enough then localized flooding would \nbe a concern. Soils continue to dry out therefore flash flood \nguidance is high.\n\nTemps look to heat up into the upper 90s to around 100 again on\nSunday with heat indicies around 100-110 degrees and the \nExtreme Heat Warning continues. \n\nHeat Continues Next Week: For Sunday through Wednesday, there \nis less confidence in the pattern as the ridge looks to be close\nto the area to where temps may surge back up, especially mid \nweek. However we may remain close enough that precip chances \nincrease. At this point, the Extreme Heat Warning most likely\nwill not be needed Monday and Tuesday however we will need to\nmonitor temp trends south of I-44 where heat indicies will be\nhighest. Wednesday looks to feature the next warmest day with\nhighs in the upper 90s to around 100 and the NWS Heat Risk tool\nshowing an increased area of \"Major\" risk for heat sensitive\ngroups.\n\nAfter Wednesday there seems to be a little more confidence\nas ensembles are in better agreement that the ridge will slide \nwest of the area, back towards the Rockies. This would place our\narea in northwest flow aloft with the potential for highs to\ndrop closer to average and perhaps increased precip chances.",
